--- !u!114 &81163085341366681
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "URP's rectangle (area) lights work only with baked lighting, so the fluorescent fixtures are made from <b>spot</b> and <b>point</b> lights. Each fixture gets three lights placed at the fixture: an Area Light and a Tube Light under <i>9 \xB7 Lights \u203A Canopy \u203A Fixture n Lights</i>, and a Fog Spot, which belongs to the fog layer (<i>10 \xB7 Fog \u203A Fog Spots</i>, \xA7 10). The kiosk's, the sign's and the street lamps' lights have their own parents under <i>9 \xB7 Lights</i>. Copy the first fixture's lights to the other five once they look right."
--- !u!114 &571710008769941130
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>Why a cookie on the wide spots.</b> A Spot light's brightness falls off quickly toward the edge of its cone. A flat panel of light (a fluorescent fixture) instead sends a lot of light sideways. The cookie <i>Textures/URP/Lambert-Spot-170.png</i> (<i>Texture Type: Cookie</i>) makes a 170\xB0 spot fade gradually from its centre to its edge, like a panel. With the inner angle almost equal to the outer one (169\xB0), the cookie alone decides the falloff."
